      "question_text": "(a) \nExplain how a named indicator organism could be used to detect pollution in an area.\n[3]\nName of indicator organism:\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \nExplanation:\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \nFigure 4 below shows the distribution of two forms of peppered moths in woodlands of the UK in \nthe 1950s. At the time, surfaces of most trees in Central and South Eastern England were heavily \nblackened with deposits of carbon from local coal burning. There are two forms of the moth, dark \nand light, and both may be eaten by woodland birds. \nFigure 4\nPrevailing \nwind\nKey:\nLight\nDark\nN\n0\n100 200\nkm\n[Source: © International Baccalaureate Organization 2014]\n(This question continues on the following page)\n16EP10\n\n– 11 –\nTurn over\nN13/4/ENVSO/SP1/ENG/TZ0/XX\n(Question 4 continued)\n \n(b) \n(i) \nDescribe the relationship between the distribution of the two forms of moths and \nhabitat pollution.\n[1]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n \n(ii) \nSuggest one reason to explain the relationship you have identified in (b)(i).\n[1]\